# Line endings, pinned explicitly.
#
# This project is developed on Windows and released from Linux and macOS, and
# a mismatch here is not cosmetic: a shell script with CRLF fails on Linux with
# "$'\r': command not found", and a Go source file with CRLF makes gofmt
# rewrite every line. core.autocrlf is a per-machine setting, so it is not a
# guarantee anyone else can rely on. These rules are.

# Normalise on commit and check out with LF for everything not listed below.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Source and build files are LF everywhere, in the repository and in the
# working tree, so the two platforms see byte-identical files.
*.go       text eol=lf
*.sh       text eol=lf
*.mod      text eol=lf
*.sum      text eol=lf
Makefile   text eol=lf
*.md       text eol=lf
*.yml      text eol=lf
*.yaml     text eol=lf
*.json     text eol=lf
.gitignore text eol=lf
LICENSE    text eol=lf
NOTICE     text eol=lf

# PowerShell and the Windows command shell are the one exception. Their
# here-strings and batch parsing are line-ending sensitive, and CRLF is what
# those tools are built around, so these stay CRLF.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binaries: never touched by the text filters, or a "conversion" would corrupt
# them.
*.exe  binary
*.dll  binary
*.so   binary
*.dylib binary
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.webp binary
*.pdf  binary
*.zip  binary
*.gz   binary
*.tgz  binary

# A generated file that would otherwise dominate every diff and the language
# statistics for the repository.
go.sum linguist-generated=true
